# Unified DomainOrder + advanced execution

Scoped rule for the Phase 2 (migration 0041) unification of the legacy
``OrderRequest`` / ``OrderData`` stack with the rich
:class:`aqp.core.domain.orders.DomainOrder` hierarchy.

Narrative walkthrough lives in
[aqp_docs/order-types.md](mdc:aqp_docs/order-types.md),
[aqp_docs/contingency-graphs.md](mdc:aqp_docs/contingency-graphs.md), and
[aqp_docs/execution-paths.md](mdc:aqp_docs/execution-paths.md).

## Hard rules

1. **``DomainOrder`` is the canonical wire shape.** New consumers
   (Phase 2+) MUST accept :class:`DomainOrder` and emit
   :class:`ExecutionReport`. Legacy ``OrderRequest`` is still
   accepted but routed through
   :class:`aqp.trading.execution.legacy_adapter.LegacyDomainOrderAdapter`.

2. **Order subclass selection is by ``order_type``.** Don't reach
   into the dataclass and override ``order_type`` after construction --
   pick the right subclass (``MarketOrder``, ``LimitOrder``,
   ``StopMarketOrder``, ``StopLimitOrder``, ``MarketIfTouchedOrder``,
   ``LimitIfTouchedOrder``, ``TrailingStopMarketOrder``,
   ``TrailingStopLimitOrder``) and let it set the type.

3. **Every order MUST pass ``validate_flags()`` before submission.**
   Brokers + the contingency manager call this at submit time and
   reject the order if any violation is reported. The exclusive flag
   pairs are ``reduce_only`` + ``close_position`` and
   ``post_only`` + ``OrderType.MARKET`` (post_only requires a limit
   order; market post_only is a contradiction).

4. **WebSocket execution paths are the primary route on supported
   venues.** :attr:`IDomainBrokerage.supports_websocket_amend` declares
   the capability. The :class:`AmendmentManager` falls back to cancel
   + resubmit when the WS amend fails OR when policy disallows the
   field. Don't bypass the manager -- it owns the atomic request id
   counter that keeps amendments deduplicated.

5. **Contingency graphs (OCO / OUO / OTO) route through
   :class:`ContingencyManager`.** When the venue supports atomic OCO
   submission, the broker still registers the ``OrderList`` with the
   manager (it's idempotent) so the manager has the audit trail. The
   manager is the only path that emits cross-order cancel / amend /
   submit commands; don't hand-roll an "if filled, cancel peer" loop
   in a strategy module.

6. **Execution reports key on ``(venue, venue_execution_id)``.** This
   is the deterministic claim mapping algorithm's natural key (Nautilus
   issues #4012 / #3176). Don't mint client UUIDs for venue-emitted
   events; the unique index on ``execution_reports`` will reject the
   row.

7. **The ``orders`` and ``fills`` legacy tables are NOT removed.**
   Phase 2 runs both stacks side by side. The
   :class:`LegacyDomainOrderAdapter` keeps them in sync. Phase 3 / 4 /
   5 don't introduce new readers against ``orders`` / ``fills``; all
   new code uses ``domain_orders`` / ``execution_reports``.

8. **Brokers MUST set ``venue`` on every emitted
   :class:`ExecutionReport`.** Without it the reconciliation engine in
   Phase 3 can't index by ``(venue, venue_execution_id)``. The legacy
   adapter sets ``venue`` from the broker's ``self.venue`` attribute.

9. **Persistence writes from the execution-report dispatcher are
   idempotent via the unique index.** Duplicate
   ``(venue, venue_execution_id)`` rows are silently swallowed
   (logged at DEBUG). This closes the WS-vs-REST race documented in
   the report.

## Cross-references

- AGENTS rule 4 -- progress events through ``_progress.emit``;
  execution reports go through the dispatcher, not Redis directly.
- AGENTS rule 5 -- order state lives in Postgres; the contingency
  manager is in-memory but its commands are persisted.
- AGENTS rule 34 -- every new ``domain_orders`` and
  ``execution_reports`` row carries ``experiment_id``.
- Migration 0041 added the three core tables; Phase 3 migration 0042
  adds the accounts surface that closes the loop.
